# ArgonOS - Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-2 (SKU 29667/29668).
#
#   argon target esp32s3-lcd2
#
# An overlay on sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3, which is the primary target and the
# one QEMU runs.  What differs is a real board's flash size and partition
# layout, and one question about the console at the bottom.

# 16 MB, NOT 8.  sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3 says in its own comment that it
# assumes an N16R8 module - 16 MB flash, 8 MB octal PSRAM - and then sets
# CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_8MB, which is what the emulator and the S3-CAM
# board have been built with.  This board's part really is 16 MB: the vendor's
# own generated sdkconfig for their examples says CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE
# "16MB".  Left at 8 the top half of the part would be unreachable, and the
# partition table below is laid out into that half.
#
# The 8MB -> 16MB override is written the way sdkconfig.esp32s3.zero writes its
# 8MB -> 4MB one: later files in SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS win, but only if the symbol
# being turned off is named as well.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_8MB=n
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions_16mb_lcd2.csv"

# PSRAM is left exactly as the primary target has it - octal mode, 80 MHz,
# SPIRAM_USE_CAPS_ALLOC - and that is this board rather than a coincidence: the
# module is an R8, 8 MB of OCTAL PSRAM, and the vendor's examples confirm it
# (CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT in their generated sdkconfig).  The pack's [display]
# leans on it: the 320x240 soft framebuffer comes out of PSRAM, not out of the
# internal SRAM the system lives in.
#
# The 192 KB arena, the 32 KB instruction cache, 240 MHz and MEMPROT off all
# come from the primary target untouched.  This board would take the S3-CAM's
# PSRAM arena (CONFIG_ARGON_APP_ARENA_PSRAM, sdkconfig.esp32s3.board) if it ever
# had to run both radios and a full-size application at once; that is a decision
# for the day a radio is switched on here, not for the day the pack is written.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NETWORKING - MEASURED: THE PRIMARY TARGET'S QEMU PATH STOPS THIS BOARD.
#
# sdkconfig.defaults, which is the primary esp32s3 target, turns on
# CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_NET together with CONFIG_ETH_USE_OPENETH, because that
# target is QEMU and its networking is the emulated OpenEth device.  On real
# silicon that MAC does not exist and the devices stage blocks in
# ag_net_init(): measured on this unit 2026-09-21, the boot prints as far as
#
#   I (39) input: joy0 ready (pad layer)
#
# and then nothing for thirty seconds - no `dev: N devices`, no media stage, no
# reset, no watchdog line.  The S3-Zero pack walked into the same wall and wrote
# the answer down, but this overlay did not inherit it: it layers on
# sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3 alone, and that fix lives in
# sdkconfig.esp32s3.zero.
#
# So the same real-radio path is taken here.  With ARGON_NET_WIFI set the port
# brings up the radio rather than OpenEth, and it starts nothing at boot unless
# [wifi]/[ap] in BOARD.CFG (or `wifi on`) asks for it - which this pack never
# does, so the board boots headless and pays no RAM for a network it is not on.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_NET=y
CONFIG_ARGON_NET_WIFI=y
CONFIG_ARGON_NET_WIFI_AP=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CODE ARENA MOVES TO PSRAM - THE PRICE OF THE RADIO, MEASURED.
#
# The section above is what this board needed to boot at all; this is what it
# needed to boot WITH a network.  Turning the real-radio path on pulls the
# Wi-Fi stack's DRAM in, and with the primary target's 192 KB arena in internal
# SRAM the link failed on this unit, 2026-09-21:
#
#   ld: region `dram0_0_seg' overflowed by 42416 bytes
#
# On this chip instruction RAM and DMA-capable data RAM are one physical SRAM,
# which is what makes the arena and a radio compete for the same bytes.
#
# The day this would be needed, and the answer, were already written down above:
# the S3-CAM's PSRAM arena (sdkconfig.esp32s3.board).  PSRAM reaches the
# instruction bus on this S3 and this board has 8 MB of it, so the arena moves
# out of the way - 32 KB stays internal as a fallback, 512 KB of code space
# comes from PSRAM, and the internal SRAM it used to hold goes to the radio.
# The cost is speed on a cache miss, not capability.  Measured after the move:
# the firmware links and the board boots with the radio path compiled in; the
# radio itself still starts only when something asks for it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_ARGON_APP_ARENA_KB=32
CONFIG_ARGON_APP_ARENA_PSRAM=y
CONFIG_ARGON_APP_ARENA_PSRAM_KB=512

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONSOLE - MEASURED ON THIS UNIT, AND IT IS THE CHIP'S OWN USB.
#
# The board has one Type-C socket, and the vendor pin map gives two paths on
# this board: UART0 on GPIO 43/44, and the chip's own native USB on GPIO 19/20.
# Which of the two the socket carries is not written down anywhere I found, and
# the vendor's own generated sdkconfig does not settle it either - it keeps
# IDF's default of UART0 with the USB-Serial-JTAG as a SECOND endpoint, which
# prints over either wiring.  ArgonOS has no secondary console yet (the primary
# target's defaults say so), so the pack has to choose one.
#
# It first chose UART0, and that choice does not work here.  Measured on this
# unit, 2026-09-21: esptool reports "USB mode: USB-Serial/JTAG", so the socket
# IS the chip's own USB; the IDF boot log comes through it (the peripheral
# bridges UART0 to USB), and the first ArgonOS image built for a UART0 console
# then came up with no banner and no prompt at all.  With the project's own
# bring-up switch (ARGON_BOOT_TRACE=1) the boot reaches
#
#   argon-trace: kernel entry
#   argon-trace: console init 80x25
#   argon-trace: attaching uart0
#   argon-trace: uart attached
#
# and from there the wire carries nothing: no banner, no prompt, no watchdog
# reset, no reboot.  The console attaches and the take-over of UART0 is what
# kills the path - the port reprograms the divider and the clock source
# (uart_hw.c, which drains the shift register first), and the USB-Serial-JTAG
# bridge does not follow that reconfiguration.  So this is not a hang in the
# kernel; it is the console that stops being readable.
#
# Hence the move that was written down here for exactly this day, the way
# sdkconfig.esp32s3.zero does it: the console is the chip's own USB, and
# AG_PORT_UART_CONSOLE becomes the JTAG pseudo-port (port/impl/uart.h) - no
# GPIOs, nothing to reconfigure, nothing to wedge.  uart0's pads 43/44 are then
# free, which is what BOARD.CFG's wire discussion assumes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_UART_DEFAULT is not set
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y
